{#- This week's Torah portion, with the reading itself inside it.

    The weekly's anatomy, and deliberately so: a pitch, then the product working on the
    thing the visitor came for. What differs is what the two are for. The news weekly
    catches somebody learning to read a newspaper; this catches somebody who is going to
    hear this portion read on Shabbat and would like to follow it.

    The corpus behind it is fixed — the same fifty-four, forever — so nothing on this
    page is generated week to week. A calendar says which portion, and the page points
    at a reader that was built once. -#}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ page_language|default('en') }}">
<head>
{% include '_public_head.html.j2' %}
<style>{{ asset('reader.css') }}{{ asset('public.css') }}{{ asset('front.css') }}</style>
{{ hebrew_face(true) }}
{{ chrome_face() }}
<script>{{ asset('keep.js') }}</script>
</head>
<body class="public front">
{#- The front door's bar (2026-09-27): the landing's places, the language, Sign in, and
    the waitlist as the one call to action. -#}
{% with home = '/', here = '/parasha' if this_week else '/parasha/' ~ portion.slug %}{% include '_front_bar.html.j2' %}{% endwith %}
<main class="sheet weekly parasha">
{#- A language the visitor pressed for rides on every link that stays on these pages,
    as `_asked` promises: a Russian reader who chose Russian is not handed the next
    portion in English (QA, 2026-10-05). -#}
{%- set kept = ('lang=' ~ asked) if asked else '' %}

  {#- The hero, as the front door draws it (2026-09-27): the headline and the waitlist on
      one side, the picture on the other as a card on the desk.

      The picture is a photograph of a Torah scroll, which §10 used to forbid outright and
      as of 2026-09-01 allows on a content surface — see §12 for the rule and its
      conditions. Nothing is set over it, so the type keeps full contrast and the picture
      keeps full strength.

      The call to action is the waitlist, and reading is the second thing: a stranger
      can read the portion below without an account, and cannot keep a word without
      one, which is what the waitlist is the way to. -#}
  <section class="front-hero">
    <div class="headline">
      {#- On the page that means "this Shabbat" the eyebrow says so; on a portion asked
          for by name it says where in the Torah it is, because that page is not about a
          week. Either way it is not the same words as the label further down. -#}
      <p class="eyebrow">{% if this_week %}{{ t("parasha.page.this-shabbat", "This Shabbat") }}{% else %}{{ books }}{% endif %}</p>
      {#- The headline names what the page is actually showing. On a portion asked for
          by name, "this week's parasha" was a lie — and fifty-two pages sharing one
          headline is also fifty-two pages a search engine cannot tell apart. -#}
      <h1>{% if this_week %}{{ t("parasha.page.pitch-this-week", "This week's parasha, every word explained.") }}{% else %}{{ t("daily.page.pitch", "{name}, every word explained.", name=portion_named) }}{% endif %}</h1>
      {% if portion.hebrew or portion.name %}
      <span class="named" {% if portion.hebrew %}lang="he" dir="rtl"{% else %}lang="{{ page_language }}" dir="ltr"{% endif %}>{{ portion.hebrew or portion_named }}</span>
      {% endif %}
      <p class="lede">
        {{ t("parasha.page.the-hebrew-as-the-masorah-wrote-it", "The Hebrew as the Masorah wrote it, with the chanting marks or without, a
      translation beside every verse, and any word you tap remembered.") }}
      </p>
      {#- The waitlist is for a stranger. Somebody who has chosen targum is not sold
          to again (§6), so for them the Read button leads the hero (copy audit,
          2026-09-28). -#}
      {%- if not signed_in %}
      {% with note = true %}{% include '_join_form.html.j2' %}{% endwith %}
      {%- endif %}
      {#- The name is Hebrew inside an English sentence, so it is isolated wherever it
          appears: without that the bidi algorithm drags the surrounding punctuation to
          the wrong end and the button reads backwards. -#}
      <p class="actions">
        <a class="btn {{ 'cta' if signed_in else 'tonal' }}" href="#embed">{{ t("parasha.page.read", "Read") }} <bdi {% if portion.hebrew %}lang="he" dir="rtl"{% else %}lang="{{ page_language }}" dir="ltr"{% endif %}>{{ portion.hebrew or portion_named }}</bdi></a>
      </p>
      {%- if signed_in %}
      {%- with series_id = 'parasha', says = t("subs.series.portion-says", "Each week's portion shows on Your targums and comes in your daily mail.") %}{% include '_series_subscribe.html.j2' %}{% endwith %}
      {%- endif %}
      {#- The week's sheet on paper (targum-internal#415), set on the box when pressed. A
          stranger here has no reader open to say how they read, so the few choices that
          change the paper are asked beside the button, each as the reader opens: their
          language beside the verse, the vowels and the te'amim, meanings over the
          harder words — or, signed in, over the words they are learning — and the
          haftarah after. A box left empty is sent as a 0 (the hidden field before it),
          because a form sends nothing for an unticked box. Onkelos and Rashi join the
          companions as the shelf carries them (#414). -#}
      <form class="sheet-choices" action="{{ sheet_action }}" method="get">
        {% for name, value in sheet_keep %}<input type="hidden" name="{{ name }}" value="{{ value }}">{% endfor %}
        <fieldset>
          <legend class="sr-only">{{ t("parasha.sheet.choose", "What the PDF shows") }}</legend>
          <input type="hidden" name="with" value="">
          <label><input type="checkbox" name="with" value="en"{% if sheet_translation == 'en' %} checked{% endif %}> {{ t("parasha.sheet.english", "English") }}</label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" name="with" value="ru"{% if sheet_translation == 'ru' %} checked{% endif %}> {{ t("parasha.sheet.russian", "Russian") }}</label>
          {#- Onkelos and Rashi on, Rashi in English off: the reader's own defaults
              (targum-internal#414), so the PDF from here is the page a reader opens. -#}
          <label><input type="checkbox" name="with" value="targum" checked> {{ t("parasha.sheet.onkelos", "Onkelos") }}</label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" name="with" value="rashi" checked> {{ t("parasha.sheet.rashi", "Rashi") }}</label>
          <label><input type="checkbox" name="with" value="rashi-en"> {{ t("parasha.sheet.rashi-english", "Rashi · English") }}</label>
          <input type="hidden" name="vowels" value="0">
          <label><input type="checkbox" name="vowels" value="1" checked> {{ t("parasha.sheet.vowels", "Vowels") }}</label>
          <input type="hidden" name="taamim" value="0">
          <label><input type="checkbox" name="taamim" value="1" checked> {{ t("parasha.sheet.taamim", "Chanting marks") }}</label>
          <input type="hidden" name="gloss" value="0">
          <label><input type="checkbox" name="gloss" value="1" checked> {{ t("parasha.sheet.gloss", "Meanings above words") }}</label>
          <input type="hidden" name="haftarah" value="0">
          <label><input type="checkbox" name="haftarah" value="1" checked> {{ t("parasha.sheet.haftarah", "Haftarah") }}</label>
        </fieldset>
        <button type="submit" class="btn tonal sheet">{{ t("parasha.page.download-pdf", "Download PDF") }}</button>
      </form>
    </div>
    {#- Decoration: it says nothing the words have not said, so it is hidden from a
        screen reader rather than described to one. -#}
    <figure class="art" aria-hidden="true">
      <img src="{{ data_uri('scroll/columns.jpg') }}" alt="">
    </figure>
  </section>

  <h2 class="label">{{ t("parasha.page.the-reading", "The reading") }}</h2>
  {#- The dateline says which portion, when, and how much of it — the four things
      somebody preparing checks before they start. -#}
  <p class="dateline">
    {#- One sentence and one key, not four fragments (targum-internal#348): a language
        puts these in its own order, and a page assembled from "is read on" plus a comma
        plus "verses in" can only ever come out in English's.

        The name rides in a blank as markup — the Hebrew keeps its own `bdi`, and a
        `Markup` value passes through `Markup.format` unescaped while everything else a
        blank carries is escaped, which is the rule `page_words` states.

        The counts go through `tn`, because Russian has three plural forms and the teens
        are the trap: 21 стих, 22 стиха, 11 стихов. -#}
    {%- set named -%}
      {{ portion_named }}{% if portion.hebrew %} (<bdi class="named" lang="he" dir="rtl">{{ portion.hebrew }}</bdi>){% endif %}
    {%- endset -%}
    {{ t("parasha.page.dateline", "{name} is read on {when} — {summary}, {verses} in {aliyot}.",
          name=named,
          when=shabbat_said ~ ((" (" ~ hdate ~ ")") if hdate else ""),
          summary=ref(portion.summary),
          verses=tn("parasha.page.verses", portion.verses, "{n} verse", "{n} verses"),
          aliyot=tn("parasha.page.aliyot", portion.aliyot, "{n} aliyah", "{n} aliyot")) }}
    {%- if portion.kind.value == 'festival' %}
    {{ t("parasha.page.festival-instead", "A festival falls on this Shabbat, so this is what is read in its place rather than the portion the cycle would otherwise have reached.") }}
    {%- elif portion.doubled %}
    {{ t("parasha.page.two-portions", "Two portions are read together this week.") }}
    {%- endif %}
  </p>

  {#- This week's reading, part by part: the aliyot and the haftarah, each a way into its
      frame, and the ones finished since the week began said as read (targum-internal#203).
      What is left is what is not muted — no count, no share, nothing that only goes up,
      the way a verse read goes quiet in shnayim mikra. When the week began is the
      server's (`calendar.week_began`); what was finished is the reader's own record in
      this browser. Links with a target, so it works with no JavaScript: `parasha.js`
      only marks what is read and brings the frame into view. -#}
  {% if week and week.parts %}
  <ol class="week" aria-label="{{ t("parasha.page.this-week-s-reading", "This week's reading") }}">
    {%- for part in week.parts %}
    <li><a class="week-part" href="{{ part.href }}" target="{{ part.frame }}" data-began="{{ week.began }}" data-document="{{ part.document }}" data-section="{{ part.section }}" data-sections="{{ part.sections }}"><bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">{{ part.name }}</bdi><span class="read" hidden> {{ t("parasha.page.read-2", "· read") }}</span></a></li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ol>
  {% endif %}

  {#- The two ways to read it. Not levels — the text is the text — but the one choice
      that changes what is on the page: whether the chanting marks are on it. Links, so
      the page works with no JavaScript at all; `parasha.js` upgrades them to a switch
      that changes the reader in place. -#}
  <nav class="ladder taamim" aria-label="{{ t("parasha.page.the-chanting-marks", "The chanting marks") }}">
    <a href="?taamim=on{{ '&' ~ kept if kept }}" data-taamim="on"{% if taamim %} class="here" aria-current="page"{% endif %}>
      <b>{{ t("parasha.page.with-the-te-amim", "With the te'amim") }}</b>
      <span class="figure">{{ t("parasha.page.as-it-is-chanted", "as it is chanted") }}</span>
      <span class="what">{{ t("parasha.page.every-mark-the-masorah-wrote-the-vowels", "Every mark the Masorah wrote: the vowels, and the cantillation the reading is sung to.") }}</span>
    </a>
    <a href="?taamim=off{{ '&' ~ kept if kept }}" data-taamim="off"{% if not taamim %} class="here" aria-current="page"{% endif %}>
      <b>{{ t("parasha.page.vowels-only", "Vowels only") }}</b>
      <span class="figure">{{ t("parasha.page.easier-to-read", "easier to read") }}</span>
      <span class="what">{{ t("parasha.page.the-chanting-marks-taken-off-the-vowels", "The chanting marks taken off, the vowels left where they were — the plainer page for reading rather than leyning.") }}</span>
    </a>
  </nav>

  {#- Israel and the diaspora read different portions for some weeks of the year, and
      for the rest they read the same one. Two things follow. It is drawn only on the
      page that means "this Shabbat" — on a named portion there is no week to disagree
      about, and the question is not being asked. And it names both portions only when
      they are actually two: a toggle offering the same name twice is a control that
      teaches nothing. -#}
  {% if this_week and diaspora and israel %}
  {% set apart = diaspora.slug != israel.slug %}
  <p class="schedules">
    {% if apart %}
    <span class="apart">{{ t("parasha.page.israel-and-the-diaspora-are-reading-different", "Israel and the diaspora are reading different portions this week.") }}</span>
    {% endif %}
    <a class="schedule{% if schedule.value == 'diaspora' %} here{% endif %}"{% if schedule.value == 'diaspora' %} aria-current="page"{% endif %} href="/parasha?schedule=diaspora{{ '&' ~ kept if kept }}">
      {{ t("parasha.page.diaspora", "Diaspora") }}{% if apart %} · <bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">{{ diaspora.hebrew }}</bdi>{% endif %}
    </a>
    <a class="schedule{% if schedule.value == 'israel' %} here{% endif %}"{% if schedule.value == 'israel' %} aria-current="page"{% endif %} href="/parasha?schedule=israel{{ '&' ~ kept if kept }}">
      {{ t("parasha.page.israel", "Israel") }}{% if apart %} · <bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">{{ israel.hebrew }}</bdi>{% endif %}
    </a>
  </p>
  {% endif %}

  <div class="embed" id="embed">
    <button type="button" class="handle" id="embed-handle" aria-expanded="false">{{ t("parasha.page.full-screen", "Full screen") }}</button>
    {#- The first aliyah, not the contents page. A portion is built in seven sections —
        one per aliyah, which is also one chanted file per page — and `index.html` is
        then a list of seven links, which is a poor first thing to hand somebody who came
        to read. The reader's own pager goes on from here, and the contents page is a
        press away inside it. -#}
    <iframe
      name="reading"
      src="/parasha/read/{{ reading }}/reader/sec-0001.html"
      title="{{ portion_named }}"
      loading="lazy"></iframe>
  </div>

  {#- The haftarah: the second reading of the same Shabbat, from the Prophets, after
      the Torah reading the way it is read after it. Its own label and dateline, the
      shape of the reading's above, and its own frame — one section, because it is
      called up once. On the page that means "this Shabbat" this is the week's haftarah,
      which on a special Shabbat is not the portion's own, and the dateline says why;
      on a portion asked for by name it is the one the portion ordinarily has. The
      reference is said even where the text is not built, because knowing what is read
      is most of what somebody preparing needs. Hebcal's Sephardic variant is in the
      index and deliberately not here: see targum-internal#201. -#}
  {% if haftarah %}
  <h2 class="label">{{ t("parasha.page.the-haftarah", "The haftarah") }}</h2>
  <p class="dateline">
    {#- One key for the sentence, with the book's Hebrew riding in a blank as markup, as
        the portion's dateline above does (targum-internal#188). -#}
    {%- set book -%}<bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">{{ haftarah.hebrew }}</bdi>{%- endset -%}
    {%- set counted = tn("parasha.page.verses", haftarah.verses, "{n} verse", "{n} verses") -%}
    {%- if haftarah.hebrew %}
    {{ t("parasha.page.haftarah-from", "{summary} — from {book}, {verses}.", summary=ref(haftarah.summary), book=book, verses=counted) }}
    {%- else %}
    {{ t("parasha.page.haftarah-says", "{summary}, {verses}.", summary=ref(haftarah.summary), verses=counted) }}
    {%- endif %}{% if haftarah_reason %}
    {{ t("parasha.page.haftarah-instead", "Read this week in place of the portion's own: {reason}.", reason=haftarah_reason) }}{% endif %}
  </p>
  {% if haftarah_readable %}
  <div class="embed" id="haftarah">
    <button type="button" class="handle" aria-expanded="false">{{ t("parasha.page.full-screen", "Full screen") }}</button>
    <iframe
      name="haftarah"
      src="/parasha/read/{{ haftarah_reading }}/reader/{{ haftarah.opens }}"
      title="{{ t("parasha.page.haftarah-title", "The haftarah, {summary}", summary=ref(haftarah.summary)) }}"
      loading="lazy"></iframe>
  </div>
  {% endif %}
  {% endif %}

  {#- Where the year goes from here. The portion before and the one after, in the order
      they are read — and it wraps, because after וזאת הברכה comes בראשית. A festival
      reading belongs to a date rather than to the cycle and has neither, only the way to
      the whole list. -#}
  <nav class="portions" aria-label="{{ t("parasha.page.the-other-portions", "The other portions") }}">
    {% if previous %}
    <a class="prev" href="/parasha/{{ previous.slug }}{{ '?' ~ kept if kept }}"><span class="which">{{ t("parasha.page.previous", "Previous") }}</span> <bdi {% if previous.hebrew %}lang="he" dir="rtl"{% else %}lang="en" dir="ltr"{% endif %}>{{ previous.hebrew or previous.name }}</bdi></a>
    {% endif %}
    <a class="all" href="{{ all_href }}">{{ t("parasha.page.all-portions", "All portions") }}</a>
    {% if following %}
    <a class="next" href="/parasha/{{ following.slug }}{{ '?' ~ kept if kept }}"><span class="which">{{ t("parasha.page.next", "Next") }}</span> <bdi {% if following.hebrew %}lang="he" dir="rtl"{% else %}lang="en" dir="ltr"{% endif %}>{{ following.hebrew or following.name }}</bdi></a>
    {% endif %}
  </nav>

  <section class="how">
    <h2 class="label">{{ t("parasha.page.why-targum", "Why targum") }}</h2>
    <p class="section-title">{{ t("parasha.page.built-for-reading-not-for-drills", "Built for reading, not for drills.") }}</p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <b>{{ t("parasha.page.every-word-one-tap", "Every word, one tap.") }}</b>
        <span>{{ t("parasha.page.a-card-says-what-it-means-how", "A card says what it means, how common it is and how it is said; tell it how well you know the word, and it remembers.") }}</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>{{ t("parasha.page.the-marks-on-or-off", "The marks, on or off.") }}</b>
        <span>{{ t("parasha.page.the-te-amim-are-what-the-reading", "The te'amim are what the reading is chanted to and clutter if you are still learning the vowels underneath them. One press takes them off and leaves everything else where it was.") }}</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>{{ t("parasha.page.seven-aliyot-seven-sections", "Seven aliyot, seven sections.") }}</b>
        <span>{{ t("parasha.page.the-reading-is-divided-the-way-it", "The reading is divided the way it is called up, so you can go to") }} <bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">שלישי</bdi> {{ t("parasha.page.without-counting-verses-the-haftarah-follows-it", "without counting verses. The haftarah follows it, the same way.") }}</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>{{ t("parasha.page.it-follows-you", "It follows you.") }}</b>
        <span>{{ t("parasha.page.words-you-mark-here-are-marked-in", "Words you mark here are marked in everything else you read — this week's portion, next week's, and the newspaper.") }}</span>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="honest">
    <h2 class="label">{{ t("parasha.page.made-honestly", "Made honestly") }}</h2>
    {#- One key, one paragraph (targum-internal#348). The edition names inside it stay as
        they are — they are what those books are called — and the sentences around them
        are a language's own. -#}
    <p class="made">
      {{ t("parasha.page.made-honestly-says",
            "The Hebrew is the Masoretic text as it is published, cut to this reading and not edited. The English beside it is {translation}. The haftarah is cut from the Prophets on the same shelf, with the translation each book carries there: the Metsudah Tanach for Judges, Samuel and I Kings, the 1917 JPS for the rest. The division into aliyot, the haftarah for each Shabbat and the calendar behind them — including which portion Israel reads and which the diaspora reads — come from Hebcal.",
            translation=translation_said) }}
    </p>
    {#- Whose work this page is built out of. The photograph asks for nothing — it is
        CC0 — and is credited anyway, which is the rule the recordings already follow:
        a credit that lives only in a repository is a credit nobody reading ever sees.
        The recording's licence does require it. -#}
    {#- Four keys became one. Each of the old ones was a fragment around a link — two of
        them began with a comma — and a fragment can only ever be put back in English's
        order. The links ride in blanks as `Markup`, which `Markup.format` passes through
        while escaping everything else (targum-internal#348). -#}
    {%- set photograph -%}
      <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Closeup_of_Torah_Scroll.jpg" rel="noopener noreferrer">{{ t("parasha.page.photographed-by-jacob-gucker", "photographed by Jacob Gucker") }}</a>
    {%- endset -%}
    {%- set recording -%}
      <a href="https://archive.org/details/PockettorahAudioFiles" rel="noopener noreferrer">PocketTorah</a>
    {%- endset -%}
    {%- set licence -%}
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>
    {%- endset -%}
    <p class="made credits">
      {{ t("parasha.page.credits-say",
            "The scroll at the top was {photograph}, and the photograph is in the public domain under CC0. Where a reading has a recording, it is {recording}, chanted in the Avery-Binder trope and used under {licence}.",
            photograph=photograph, recording=recording, licence=licence) }}
    </p>

    {% if listed %}
    {#- Every portion, all year, because the corpus is fixed and there is no reason to
        make somebody wait a year to read one. Folded on a phone. -#}
    <details class="sources" id="sources" open>
      <summary><b class="sub">{{ t("parasha.page.every-portion", "Every portion") }} <span class="count">({{ listed|length }})</span></b></summary>
      <ul class="portions">
        {%- for one in listed %}
        <li>
          <a class="story" href="/parasha/{{ one.slug }}{{ '?' ~ kept if kept }}" {% if one.hebrew %}lang="he" dir="rtl"{% else %}lang="{{ page_language }}" dir="ltr"{% endif %}>{{ one.hebrew or name_of(one) }}</a>
          <span class="row">
            <span class="outlets" lang="{{ page_language }}" dir="ltr">{{ name_of(one) }}</span>
            <span class="licence">{{ ref(one.summary) }}</span>
          </span>
        </li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ul>
    </details>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {#- The door is the waitlist (2026-09-27): a stranger who has read this far has no
      account to sign in to. The landing's own section, from the same file. -#}
  {%- if not signed_in %}
  {% include '_front_join.html.j2' %}
  {%- endif %}
</main>

{% if strings %}<script>window.TARGUM_STRINGS = {{ strings|tojson }};</script>
{% endif %}<script>{{ asset('strings.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('parasha.js') }}</script>

{% include '_public_foot.html.j2' %}
</body>
</html>
